detail.abstract:
Purpose-This study investigates whether top management ethical leadership and green innovation affect green supply chain management (GSCM), environmental performance (EP), and their relationship. While much prior research has focused solely on observable characteristics, this study addresses underexplored psychological aspects by examining ethical leadership.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach-Our framework is developed by applying the natural resource-based view and upper echelons theory in the context of GSCM, with two mediators: green product innovation (GPI) and green process innovation (GPrI). The framework is validated using a global sample of 351 top management respondents, focusing on EP using PLS-SEM.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings-We find that top management ethical leadership positively impacts GSCM practices and green innovation activities, which, in turn, enhance the EP of firms. In addition, post hoc comparisons among high, medium, and low environmental-impact industry groups show different relationships between GSCM practices and different kinds of EP.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/value-This study contributes to research in this area. First, we incorporate top management's ethical leadership as a managerial driver of GSCM and EP. Our study shows that leadership-initiated green practices enhance EP, using GPI and GPrI as mediators in an integrated model. Second, we develop and validate a measurement scale for EP based on the GRI standard. Our categorization of EP consists of three main categories, whereas past studies have examined green performance mostly as a single dimension of overall performance. In addition, we evaluate our model across low-and high-impact industries for actionable insights concerning green practices customization.
</div>
